% ============================================================================
% lunar-eclipse-type — three named lunar eclipse types and what each
% actually is (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A lunar eclipse happens when Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon,
% but not every lunar eclipse looks the same: whether the Moon moves fully
% into Earth's dark inner shadow, only partway into it, or only through
% Earth's faint outer shadow depends on how exactly the three bodies line
% up. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? lunar_eclipse_type(total_lunar_eclipse, $D)
%       % the_moon_moves_into_the_inner_part_of_earths_shadow_the_umbra
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `lunar_eclipse_type(type, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on an eclipse type that is not one of these
% three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the
% eclipse type it names:
%
%     ? lunar_eclipse_type($T, the_moon_travels_through_earths_penumbra_the_faint_outer_part_of_its_shadow)
%       % penumbral_eclipse
%
% The three eclipse types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                     description                                                                       quote (verbatim)
%     -----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     total_lunar_eclipse       the_moon_moves_into_the_inner_part_of_earths_shadow_the_umbra                       "The Moon moves into the inner part of Earth’s shadow, or the umbra."
%     partial_lunar_eclipse     an_imperfect_alignment_of_sun_earth_and_moon_results_in_partial_umbra_passage       "An imperfect alignment of Sun, Earth and Moon results in the Moon passing through only part of Earth's umbra."
%     penumbral_eclipse         the_moon_travels_through_earths_penumbra_the_faint_outer_part_of_its_shadow         "The Moon travels through Earth’s penumbra, or the faint outer part of its shadow."
%
% APOSTROPHES ABOVE ARE MIXED ON PURPOSE -- DO NOT NORMALIZE THEM. The NASA
% page renders the total and penumbral sentences with U+2019 (’) and the
% partial sentence with an ASCII apostrophe (').  <- those two exemplars
% are DIFFERENT characters; if your editor shows them as the same, it is
% doing the very normalization this note exists to prevent. Each quote above was checked
% against a rendered block on the page individually; a blanket curl would make
% the partial quote stop appearing on its own page. See #14070.
%
% Provenance: quoted from NASA's "Eclipses and the Moon" page, which
% defines each of these three lunar eclipse types in its own single
% sentence, followed by a separate elaboration sentence not folded into
% the quote. (This used to say each is the "opening" sentence of its
% paragraph; the penumbral one is not, as noted below.) The original
% authoring note said the quotes were "WebFetch-verified twice"; the
% raw-page measurement below supersedes it. `trust authoritative` — NASA
% is a primary .gov source, the
% same tier the sibling `solar-eclipse-type.adj` (a sibling library in this
% directory) already uses for its NASA citation.
%
% Honest abstention on `blood_moon`: a real, well-documented term the SAME
% page discusses under its own '"Blood Moon": How It Works' heading, but
% the page uses it as a NICKNAME for the reddish color a total lunar
% eclipse produces, not as a fourth peer eclipse type alongside these
% three. So a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a peer-level row
% for a color phenomenon. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the total row's own sentence, the primary source of all three
% answers. Each quoted sentence above names what its type is, so every row
% now takes its own as a `source`, with the apostrophes the note above
% describes. The envelope is now a framing sentence from the page, which
% names no type:
%
%     "Lunar eclipses occur at the full Moon phase."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row sentences and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. The total and partial sentences open
% their paragraphs; the penumbral one follows "If you don’t know this one
% is happening, you might miss it." Every row's page is the envelope's, so
% no row restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4). The
% blood-moon heading above used to be quoted with single quotes; the page
% writes double quotes (in its `<h2>` and its table-of-contents link), and
% the quote now does too.
% ============================================================================

table lunar_eclipse_type {
    columns type, description

    row (total_lunar_eclipse, the_moon_moves_into_the_inner_part_of_earths_shadow_the_umbra) {
        source "The Moon moves into the inner part of Earth’s shadow, or the umbra."
    }
    row (partial_lunar_eclipse, an_imperfect_alignment_of_sun_earth_and_moon_results_in_partial_umbra_passage) {
        source "An imperfect alignment of Sun, Earth and Moon results in the Moon passing through only part of Earth's umbra."
    }
    row (penumbral_eclipse, the_moon_travels_through_earths_penumbra_the_faint_outer_part_of_its_shadow) {
        source "The Moon travels through Earth’s penumbra, or the faint outer part of its shadow."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE TOTAL ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no type.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "Lunar eclipses occur at the full Moon phase."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/"
    trust authoritative
}
